GUIDE = "# Rollover Execution Playbook\n**Internal advisor checklist | October 2026 | For licensed, authorized NYL personnel**\n\nThis guide is based on the supplied 38-page training transcript, with additional regulatory and execution safeguards. It is not NYL-approved advertising, legal/tax advice, or a substitute for current NYL/NYLIFE Securities procedures. Never prioritize compensation over the client's best interest.\n\n## 1. Qualify the opportunity\n- [ ] Confirm client identity, state of residence, registration/appointment and your authority to discuss securities or annuities.\n- [ ] Identify triggering event: separation, retirement, plan termination, eligible in-service distribution, or existing IRA transfer.\n- [ ] Obtain current statement and plan distribution summary; identify account type (pre-tax, Roth, after-tax), balance, employer stock, loans, vesting, fees and beneficiaries.\n- [ ] Identify liquidity needs, emergency savings, age, employment, tax bracket, planned retirement, risk capacity and tolerance, investment objectives, time horizon, other assets and outside advice.\n- [ ] Identify potential RMDs, age-55 separation exception, age-59½ access, creditor protection, NUA employer-stock treatment, outstanding plan loans, and special 403(b)/governmental plan rules. Escalate unusual cases.\n\n## 2. Present ALL available alternatives\n- [ ] Leave money in the existing employer plan (when allowed): compare investment menu, fees, services, protections and withdrawal features.\n- [ ] Roll into the new employer's plan (if accepted): compare costs, loans, consolidation, access and plan restrictions.\n- [ ] Direct rollover to an IRA: compare investments, ongoing service, costs, creditor treatment, withdrawal exceptions and suitability.\n- [ ] Take a taxable distribution: explain ordinary income taxes, withholding and potential additional 10% tax before 59½, subject to exceptions.\n- [ ] Consider Roth conversion separately only after tax analysis; a pre-tax-to-Roth conversion generally creates taxable income.\n- [ ] Document why the recommendation is better for THIS client than leaving assets where they are, including any increased fees, lost plan features and conflicts.\n\n## 3. Client discovery and suitability\n- [ ] Send the NYL investor profile / IPT questionnaire for independent client completion. Do not coach responses or suggest a higher risk score.\n- [ ] Obtain recent plan statement and any required plan documentation through approved secure channels.\n- [ ] Review existing holdings and proposed allocation with authorized tools, including Morningstar RBT where applicable (transcript process; confirm current access).\n- [ ] Prepare side-by-side comparison: current plan vs proposed IRA vs proposed annuity. Include all-in costs, fund expenses, advisory fees, sales loads, annuity M&E/rider charges, surrender schedule, liquidity and guarantees.\n- [ ] For variable annuity within IRA, explicitly justify extra insurance features: the IRA already provides tax deferral. Evaluate contract surrender restrictions and investment risk. Never claim all fund exchanges are free of charges.\n- [ ] Discuss compensation/conflicts accurately using current disclosures; never claim a transfer is 'free' without checking all fees.\n\n## 4. Prepare and obtain approval\n- [ ] Confirm state securities registration, product licensing, firm permissions and supervision before soliciting or recommending.\n- [ ] Complete firm-required rollover/best-interest documentation and supervisory review BEFORE processing.\n- [ ] Verify exact product and account availability, share class, breakpoints, expense ratios and applicable prospectus.\n- [ ] Complete electronic disclosures through current NYL portal: privacy notice, Form CRS, applicable relationship guide, prospectus and state/product-specific documents. Transcript references CRG and form 24215; verify current forms, applicability and timing.\n- [ ] For a brokerage IRA, confirm current SalesCentral investments eApp process. For an annuity, confirm current eAnnuity workflow. Do not assume transcript screens remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Confirm account registration and beneficiaries, withholding elections if applicable, and exact payee/receiving account instructions.\n\n## 5. Move funds: prevent delays and tax errors\n- [ ] Call current plan administrator with client authorization to determine whether direct rollover is eligible, forms, signature/medallion requirements, processing windows and fees.\n- [ ] Prefer a properly executed DIRECT rollover from plan to eligible receiving IRA when appropriate; checks may be made payable to the receiving custodian FBO client. Follow official payee instructions.\n- [ ] Distinguish a direct rollover from an indirect rollover. Indirect rollovers generally have a 60-day deadline and may involve mandatory 20% withholding on eligible employer-plan distributions paid to the participant; exceptions and restrictions apply.\n- [ ] Confirm whether the destination can receive in-kind securities; many employer plans liquidate before distribution. Do not promise an in-kind transfer.\n- [ ] Track submission date, expected disbursement, check/wire tracking, receipt, good order and investment allocation; never route client money through your personal account.\n- [ ] Escalate stale requests and missing paperwork promptly, with client permission. Never pressure a client to transfer before comparison and approval.\n\n## 6. Close the loop after funding\n- [ ] Verify cleared assets, correct tax character and investment instructions; document receipt and execution.\n- [ ] Confirm client online access, beneficiaries, initial holdings, fees and service contacts.\n- [ ] Schedule 30-day service review and annual retirement-plan review; coordinate RMDs and tax matters with CPA.\n- [ ] Record consent-based referrals or introductions only under NYL-approved rules.\n- [ ] Check firm compensation records separately. No commission is earned merely because an application is signed; payout depends on eligible product, funding, supervision, chargebacks and firm schedule.\n\n## 7. Deal-progress checklist\n**Discovery:** client consent / eligibility / latest statement / goals / current costs.\n**Recommendation:** alternatives compared / best-interest rationale / client understanding / supervisory approval.\n**Application:** accurate account setup / signed forms / disclosures delivered / receiving instructions confirmed.\n**Transfer:** current custodian contacted / request submitted / funds tracked / exception escalated.\n**Funded:** receipt verified / investments allocated / confirmation delivered / follow-up booked.\n**Compensation:** eligible transaction confirmed in official NYL records / no undisclosed conflicts.\n\n## 8. Suggested language\n**Opening:** “When you leave an employer, there are usually several ways to handle your retirement plan. My job is to compare them with you, including the option of leaving it where it is, so you can make an informed choice.”\n\n**Statement request:** “Could you securely share your latest plan statement and any distribution information? I'll compare your current costs, investments and plan features with the alternatives.”\n\n**Funding follow-up:** “With your authorization, I'll coordinate with the plan administrator and our receiving team, confirm the correct direct-rollover instructions, and keep you informed until the assets are received.”\n\n**Fee objection:** “Let's put the current and proposed all-in costs side by side, including fund expenses, transaction or advisory fees and any annuity charges. We should only move the assets if the overall benefits justify the change.”\n\n## 9. Transcript-to-policy caution\nThe supplied training describes four options, the IPT questionnaire, Morningstar RBT, investments eApp/eAnnuity, electronic disclosures and hands-on custodian follow-up. It also includes broad claims about annuity charges, continued pretax payroll contributions to an IRA, probate, fees and tax treatment that should NOT be repeated as general rules without verification. Direct rollover is distinct from a 60-day indirect rollover. IRA contribution eligibility/limits and employer payroll deferrals are separate issues. Annuity guarantees depend on the issuing insurer and contract; variable investments can lose value.\n\n**Sources:** User-supplied “Options for handling a 401K upon separation” training transcript (Oct. 9, 2026), pp. 1–3 (four options), 20–28 (IPT, statement, Morningstar, eApp and transfer), 29–34 (fees and disclosures). General safeguards: IRS rollover guidance, FINRA Rule 2111/Reg BI where applicable, SEC Regulation Best Interest and Form CRS; verify current firm policies and laws.\n"
